<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-product-coproduct-sets" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Product and coproduct of sets</title>

  <p>We begin with the definition of product of sets. </p>
<definition xml:id="def-product-of-sets"><title>(Product of sets)</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>I</m> be a nonempty set and consider sets <m>X_\alpha</m> for every <m>\alpha\in I</m>. A <em>product of <m>X_\alpha</m></em> is a set denoted by <m>\prod X_\alpha</m> and it is `characterized' by the following properties.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          For each <m>\alpha\in I</m>, there is a map <m>\pi_\alpha\colon\prod X_\alpha\to X_\alpha</m>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If <m>Y</m> is any set together with maps <m>f_\alpha\colon Y\to X_\alpha</m> for each <m>\alpha</m>, then there is a unique map <m>g\colon Y\to\prod X_\alpha</m> satisfying <m>\pi_\alpha\circ g=f_\alpha</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      These conditions can be summarize in the following `diagram'.
    </p>
    
  </statement>
</definition>
  <figure xml:id="fig-commutative-diagram-for-product-of-sets">
  <caption>The universal property of product of sets</caption>
  <image width="60%">
  <latex-image>
\begin{tikzcd}
        \amp Y \arrow[d, dashed,"g"] \arrow[ldd, bend right,"f_\beta",labels=above left] \arrow[rdd, bend left,"f_\gamma"] \amp          \\
        \amp \prod X_\alpha \arrow[ld,"\pi_\beta"] \arrow[rd,"\pi_\gamma",labels=below left]                               \amp          \\
X_\beta \amp                                                                    \amp X_\gamma
\end{tikzcd}
    
  </latex-image>
  </image>
  </figure>

  <lemma xml:id="lem-existence-of-product-of-sets">
  <title>(Existence of a product of sets)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A product of sets <m>X_\alpha</m> is the Cartesian product of sets, i.e.,  <me>\prod X_\alpha=\lbrace(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in I}:x_\alpha\in X_\alpha\rbrace.</me> 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </lemma>
  <proof>
    <p>
      We let <m>X=\{(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in I}:x_\alpha\in X_\alpha\}</m>. We consider, for each <m>\beta\in I</m>, the projection onto <m>\beta</m>-th component of <m>X</m>, i.e., <m>\pi_\alpha\colon X\to X_\beta</m> by <m>(x_\alpha)\mapsto x_\beta</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>Y</m> be a set with maps <m>f_\alpha\colon Y\to X_\alpha</m> for each <m>\alpha\in I</m>. A map <m>g\colon Y\to X</m> satisfying <m>f_\alpha=\pi_\alpha\circ g</m> must map an element <m>y\in Y</m> to <m>(f_\alpha(y))_{\alpha\in I}\in X</m>. Hence, we get the result.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-product-of-sets">
    <statement>Find a product of <m>\{1,2\}</m> and <m>\{1,2\}</m>. Also find a product of <m>\R</m> and <m>\Z</m>.
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <lemma xml:id="lem-uniqueness-of-product-of-sets"><title>(Uniqueness of product of sets)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A product of sets is unique up to a canonical bijection of sets.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </lemma>
  <proof>
    <p>
      Proof is left as an exercise.
    </p>
  </proof>
  <remark>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <xref ref="lem-uniqueness-of-product-of-sets"/> makes precise the meaning of the word <em>characterized</em> in <xref ref="def-product-of-sets"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </remark>
  <p>
    We now define a <q>coproduct</q> of sets.
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-coproduct-of-sets"><title>(Coproduct/disjoint union of sets)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>I</m> be a nonempty indexing set, and let <m>X_\alpha</m> be sets for each <m>\alpha\in I</m>. A <em>coproduct</em> or a <em>disjoint union</em> of <m>X_\alpha</m>'s is the set <m>\coprod X_\alpha</m> with the following properties.
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              For each <m>\alpha\in I</m>, there is a map  <m>i_\alpha\colon X_\alpha\to\coprod X_\alpha</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>Y</m> is any set together with maps <m>f_\alpha\colon X_\alpha\to Y</m> for each <m>\alpha\in I</m>, then there exists a unique map <m>g\colon\coprod X_\alpha\to Y</m> such that <m>f_\alpha=g\circ i_\alpha</m> for each <m>\alpha\in I</m>, see the following diagram.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-universal-property-of-coproduct-of-sets">
        <caption>The universal property of coproduct</caption>
        <image width="60%">
        <latex-image>
\begin{tikzcd}
X_\beta \arrow[rd,"i_\beta"] \arrow[rdd, bend right,"f_\beta",labels=left] \amp  \amp X_\gamma \arrow[ld,"i_\gamma",labels=left] \arrow[ldd, bend left,"f_\gamma",labels=right] \\
\amp \coprod X_\alpha \arrow[d,dashed,"g"] \amp \\
\amp Y                          \amp                                           
\end{tikzcd}
        </latex-image>          
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <exercise>
    <title>(Existence and uniquness of a coproduct of sets)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Arguing similar to <xref ref="lem-existence-of-product-of-sets"/> and <xref ref="lem-uniqueness-of-product-of-sets"/> show that a coproduct of sets exists and it is unique up to bijection.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>Find a coproduct of <m>\{1,2\}</m> and <m>\{1,2\}</m>. Also find a coproduct of <m>\R</m> and <m>\Z</m>. Compare it with <xref ref="ex-product-of-sets"/>.
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <example xml:id="finite-prod-coprod-are-same">
  <title>(Product and coproduct of finite number of sets)</title>
  
  
    <statement>
      <p>
        If the indexing set <m>I</m> is finite, say <m>|I|=n</m> then, <m>\prod_{i=1}^nX_i=\coprod_{i=1}^nX_i</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
</section>